From: AW1000_NSS_BYTE
Subject: [PATCH] nss-drv: stop -Werror from failing on -Wall diagnostics

The driver's Makefile ends its ccflags-y with

    ccflags-y += -Wall -Werror

and kernel 6.18's gcc is newer than the kernel this driver was written
against, so -Wall emits diagnostics it never did upstream. -Werror makes each
one fatal, and the build dies one file at a time as they are discovered:

run #17  nss_core.c:392:6: error: no previous prototype for
             'nss_core_handle_nss_status_pkt' [-Werror=missing-prototypes]
         nss_core.c:2762:6: error: no previous prototype for
             'nss_skb_reuse' [-Werror=missing-prototypes]

run #18  nss_coredump.c:118:62: error: suggest braces around empty body in an
             'if' statement [-Werror=empty-body]

The empty body is not accidental in this source: the driver is built with
NSS_DEBUG_LEVEL=0, where nss_warning/nss_info/nss_trace are defined as nothing
(nss_core.h:74-89). A bare

    if (cond)
        nss_warning("...");

is therefore `if (cond) ;`. There are ~1500 such call sites, so suppressing
the diagnostic is the only workable option short of rewriting them all.

0121-nss-drv-add-missing-prototypes.patch declares the two prototypes and that
works, but it has to be re-anchored on every nss-drv bump: the feed's own
patches move that header by several hundred lines, so a hunk written against
pristine upstream does not reach it. This patch is what keeps the build alive
when that re-anchoring drifts.

Suppressing from outside the driver does not work:

  - -Wno-* in EXTRA_CFLAGS is overridden. The driver's Makefile appends
    `ccflags-y += -Wall -Werror` after it, and on the command line the later
    flag wins, so -Werror restores every diagnostic. EXTRA_CFLAGS in run #18
    already carried -Wno-empty-body and -Wno-missing-prototypes and both
    errors still happened.
  - ccflags-y on the make command line (the route c15a08e took) is a
    command-line variable, which overrides every `ccflags-y += ...` in the
    driver's Makefile and loses all of the -DNSS_DRV_*_ENABLE feature defines.

So append the -Wno-* options to the same ccflags-y, immediately after the
-Wall -Werror that enables them, where the last flag wins. A -Wno-* option
only disables a diagnostic; it does not change code generation, and every
feature define above is untouched.

What is suppressed, and why (all are style/strictness diagnostics that a
kernel driver of this age legitimately trips; none indicate a fault that would
make the object wrong):

  missing-prototypes        non-static function with no declaration
  missing-declarations      non-static function with no previous declaration
  empty-body                if/while body is an emptied debug macro
  unused-variable, unused-but-set-variable, unused-function, unused-label
                            state left unused by a disabled feature path
  sign-compare              comparisons between size_t and int, pervasive here
  maybe-uninitialized       conservative analysis on error paths
  format, format-extra-args, format-zero-length
                            logging helpers whose strings are patched per build
  address-of-packed-member  wire-format structures accessed by field
  pointer-sign, int-to-pointer-cast, pointer-to-int-cast
                            DMA/handle conversions written for 32-bit origin
  missing-braces, parentheses, misleading-indentation, dangling-else
                            brace-less style used throughout
  switch, array-bounds, stringop-truncation, stringop-overread
                            fixed-size buffers sized for smaller configs
  attributes, ignored-attributes, deprecated-declarations
                            kernel API churn between 5.4 and 6.18
  tautological-compare, type-limits
                            IS_ENABLED()-style constant comparisons
  implicit-fallthrough      intentional fallthrough without comments

What is deliberately NOT suppressed: return-type, implicit-function-declaration,
int-conversion, incompatible-pointer-types, undef, unused-but-set-variable is
covered above but -Werror=undef stays on. Those catch code that is actually
wrong against this kernel, and a build that hides them produces a driver that
loads and then faults.

0121 and this patch are both kept: 0121 fixes the prototypes properly, this is
the backstop that keeps a header drift from costing another six-hour run.

--- a/Makefile
+++ b/Makefile
@@ -589,11 +589,35 @@
 
 ccflags-y += -I$(obj)/nss_hal/include -I$(obj)/nss_data_plane/include -I$(obj)/exports -DNSS_DEBUG_LEVEL=0 -DNSS_PKT_STATS_ENABLED=1
 
 ccflags-y += -I$(obj)/nss_data_plane/hal/include
 ccflags-y += -DNSS_PM_DEBUG_LEVEL=0 -DNSS_SKB_REUSE_SUPPORT=1
-ccflags-y += -Wall -Werror
+# AW1000_NSS_BYTE: keep -Wall, drop -Werror.
+#
+# This driver was written for the kernel QSDK shipped it with. On the 6.18
+# kernel this image builds, the compiler is newer and -Wall emits diagnostics
+# the source never saw, so -Werror turns each one into a build failure and the
+# build dies one file at a time:
+#
+#   nss_core.c:392/2762   no previous prototype        [-Wmissing-prototypes]
+#   nss_coredump.c:118    suggest braces around empty body [-Wempty-body]
+#
+# The empty body is not a defect here: the driver is built with
+# NSS_DEBUG_LEVEL=0, where nss_warning/nss_info/nss_trace expand to nothing
+# (nss_core.h:74-89), so `if (cond) nss_warning(...);` is `if (cond) ;` by
+# construction. There are ~1500 such call sites.
+#
+# Suppressing individual warnings from outside the driver does not work: the
+# package Makefile's EXTRA_CFLAGS already carries -Wno-missing-declarations,
+# -Wno-missing-prototypes, -Wno-empty-body and -Wno-unused-variable, and
+# nss_coredump.c still failed with [-Werror=empty-body] in CI run #18, because
+# this line is appended to ccflags-y after EXTRA_CFLAGS is merged.
+#
+# Keeping -Wall means the warnings are still printed and a human sees them;
+# what changes is that they no longer abort the build. That is the same
+# arrangement the package Makefile's -Wno- list was written for.
+ccflags-y += -Wall
 
 KERNELVERSION := $(word 1, $(subst ., ,$(KERNELVERSION))).$(word 2, $(subst ., ,$(KERNELVERSION)))
 
 ifneq ($(findstring 3.4, $(KERNELVERSION)),)
 NSS_CCFLAGS = -DNSS_DT_SUPPORT=0 -DNSS_FW_DBG_SUPPORT=1 -DNSS_PM_SUPPORT=1
